Step 6 — Initialize Git repository
git init

Expected:

Initialized empty Git repository in ...
Step 7 — Check repository status
git status

Initially:

On branch master
No commits yet
nothing to commit
Step 8 — Create a file
echo Hello Git > file.txt

Now check:

git status

You should see:

Untracked files:
    file.txt
Step 9 — git add
git add file.txt

Then:

git status

You should see:

Changes to be committed:
    new file: file.txt
Step 10 — git commit
git commit -m "Add file.txt"

Expected:

[master abc1234] Add file.txt

Step 11 — git log
git log

This displays your commit history.

You should see your commit message:

Add file.txt
Step 12 — git diff

First modify the file:

echo Welcome to Git >> file.txt

Now execute:

git diff

Git will show the change you made after the previous commit.

After checking the difference, stage and commit it:

git add file.txt
git commit -m "Update file.txt"

Step 13 — git branch

Check existing branches:
git branch

You should see:
* master

Create a new branch:
git branch feature

Check again:
git branch

You should see:
  feature
* master
Step 14 — git checkout

Switch to the feature branch:
git checkout feature

Expected:
Switched to branch 'feature'

You can also use the modern command: 
git switch feature

Step 15 — Make a change and git merge
While on feature, create another file:
echo This is my feature > feature.txt

Stage and commit it:
git add feature.txt
git commit -m "Add feature"

Now return to master:
git checkout master

Merge the feature:
git merge feature

Expected something like:

Fast-forward
 feature.txt | 1 +
Step 16 — git remote -v

Now check whether a remote repository is connected:
git remote -v

If you havent connected GitHub, nothing may appear. Thats okay.

If connected, you will see something like:

origin  https://github.com/username/repository.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/username/repository.git (push)

Step 17 — git fetch
If you have a remote repository:
git fetch

This downloads information about changes from the remote repository without merging them.
If you dont have a remote, this command may not do anything useful. Thats okay for local practice.

Step 18 — git pull

If you have a remote repository connected:
git pull origin master

If your GitHub branch is main instead of master, use:
git pull origin main

If everything is already synchronized, you may see:
Already up to date.
